3. Having heard the learned counsel for the parties and having perused the Petition papers, this Court is inclined to grant indulgence as under and for the following reasons:
a) The jugular vein of the dispute is title to the property; learned counsel for the Petitioner submits that this property was taken by the State way back in 1960 or so;
the right to property was constitutionally guaranteed as a Fundamental Right under Article 19(1)(f) which after 42nd Amendment in 1978, has been relegated as a simple constitutional guarantee in terms of Article 300A as interpreted in K T PLANTATIONS supra; that being the position, when a citizen complains that his property has been knocked away by the State, there has to be a reasonable enquiry into the same, of course with the participation of all stakeholders.
